## (1) The work delivered

The runner's memory guard now watches the host, not one process. tools/runner gained:
  - _host_mem_bytes(): total physical memory (macOS sysctl -n hw.memsize; Linux /proc/meminfo MemTotal).
  - _host_avail_bytes(): system-wide available memory (macOS vm_stat free+inactive+purgeable+speculative with page size parsed from the header; Linux /proc/meminfo MemAvailable). Honors WEIZIGO_HOST_MEM_AVAIL_MB injection (the test hook — the danger floor is never tested by exhausting the host).
  - --host-mem-floor-mb (default 0 = derive total//8 ≈ 6 GB on 48 GB) and --no-host-guard.
  - --rss-cap-mb default raised 4096 → 12288 (secondary bound; catches a single runaway, not a legitimate 4.1 GB check).
  - In the poll loop: poll_rss collected per member; when host avail < floor, the largest member is SIGKILLed (surgical, single pid) with a two-line log naming the process, its RSS, and the avail/floor figures. If the largest is the direct child, the run ends exit 124; else the loop continues and the next poll kills the next-largest if pressure persists.
  - Progress watchdog and wall/CPU fallback ceilings untouched.

tools/regression-runner-guard.sh: four arms (null, seeded composition, seeded progress-unchanged, guard-bite), all green. Wired into build.zig's test_step (working tree; integration commit deferred — see (3)).

## (2) Hypotheses formed and ruled out

1. 'The host guard should kill the whole process group like the other guards.' RULED OUT — the brief says 'kill the largest member of the process group', i.e. a fleet citizen relieves pressure surgically and the run continues if it can. Killing the whole group on a transient host-pressure spike (e.g. a fleet sibling's peak) would kill correct work. Surgical single-pid kill, escalating to exit 124 only when the direct child is the largest.
2. 'Raise --rss-cap-mb to 8192 to match the operator's raised rows.' RULED OUT — 8192 is the operator's per-row figure for WORKERS, not a runaway bound. The 2026-07-29 zig blowup was 12.5 GB resident / 21.8 GB peak; a cap below that catches the single-runaway case the host guard is not meant for (the host guard is about FLEET composition). 12288 keeps the single-runaway guard meaningful while unblocking the 4.1 GB legitimate check.
3. 'Use memory_pressure(1) for the macOS available reading.' RULED OUT in favour of vm_stat — memory_pressure's output format is less stable across macOS versions and its percentage is a derived signal, whereas vm_stat's reclaimable page classes (free/inactive/purgeable/speculative) times the stated page size is the available-memory figure directly. vm_stat is also faster to parse and present on every darwin install.
4. 'Available = free pages only.' RULED OUT — free alone understates reclaimable memory (inactive/purgeable/speculative are reclaimable without swapping), which would fire the guard too early and kill correct work under normal memory pressure. The reclaimable sum is the honest 'available' figure.

