## (4) Uncertainties in my own deliverable

1. The host guard's 'kill the largest member' uses CURRENT RSS (poll_rss), not peak. A process whose RSS just spiked but the sample caught a low moment could be missed; conversely a process between allocations could be the 'largest' by stale current RSS. Current RSS is the right signal for 'who is holding host memory right now'; peak would over-penalise a process that already freed. I am confident in current RSS for the host-pressure purpose.
2. The default floor total//8 (6 GB on 48 GB) is a judgement call. The brief said 'derive from hw.memsize rather than hardcoding', and 1/8 leaves ~6 GB headroom — enough that watchdogd does not starve (the panic was at ~46 MB free) while not firing so early that a legitimate fleet of five 8 GB rows (40 GB, 8 GB free) trips it. --host-mem-floor-mb overrides. The panic precedent is cited in the code comment so nobody raises it casually.
3. The seeded-composition test injects a CONSTANT low host reading (WEIZIGO_HOST_MEM_AVAIL_MB=100), so the guard kills members in descending RSS order every poll until the direct child dies (exit 124). In real life, killing the largest relieves pressure and the guard stops; the test cannot show that without a dynamic injection, which would complicate the test for no extra coverage of the guard LOGIC (the logic is 'avail < floor → kill largest'; the relief behaviour is the host's, not the guard's). I am satisfied the test covers the logic the brief's bar names.
4. `zig build test` full suite exceeded the 600 s tool timeout during the engine/verify-battery modules (unrelated to T362). All T362-relevant regressions pass individually. I did not obtain a full-suite green within the session; an Orchestrator acceptance run should confirm the whole suite, but T362 touches only tools/runner + a build.zig wiring line, so engine-module failures would not be T362 regressions.

